export const MAIN_HTML = "<article class=\"hub\">\n\n<header class=\"hub-head\">\n<h1>Weather Briefing Types: Standard, Abbreviated, Outlook, and Self-Briefing</h1>\n<p class=\"byline\">PlaneWX \u00b7 Last reviewed October 1, 2026</p>\n\n<p class=\"answer\">The FAA defines three preflight weather briefings. Get an outlook briefing when departure is 6 or more hours away, a standard briefing (the complete picture) as close to departure as you can, and an abbreviated briefing just before takeoff if the standard is 1&nbsp;hour or more old or the weather is questionable. You can get them from Flight Service or brief yourself online&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 3.3.1]</a></span>&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-3\">[3, paras. 7.1 and 8.1]</a></span>.</p>\n\n<section class=\"takeaways\" aria-label=\"Weather briefing types: key takeaways\">\n<h2>Weather briefing types: key takeaways</h2>\n<ul>\n  <li>Brief in layers: outlook the day before, standard close to departure, abbreviated just before takeoff&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-3\">[3, para. 8.1]</a></span>.</li>\n  <li>Use the standard briefing order, adverse conditions first, as your self-briefing checklist&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 3.3.1.1]</a></span>.</li>\n  <li>\u201cVFR flight not recommended\u201d is advice, and the call is yours as PIC&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 3.3.1.1]</a></span>.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n</header>\n\n<aside class=\"keyfacts\" aria-label=\"Key facts about weather briefings\">\n<details class=\"kf-box\" id=\"key-facts\" open>\n<summary><h2>Weather briefing key facts</h2></summary>\n<ol>\n  <li>There are three types of preflight briefing: standard, abbreviated, and outlook&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 3.3.1]</a></span>.</li>\n  <li>A <a class=\"gl\" href=\"#gl-stdbrief\" data-gl=\"stdbrief\">standard briefing</a> is the most detailed of all briefings&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 3.3.1.1]</a></span>.</li>\n  <li>An <a class=\"gl\" href=\"#gl-outlookbrief\" data-gl=\"outlookbrief\">outlook briefing</a> is for departures 6 or more hours away, for planning only&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-3\">[3, para. 7.2.3]</a></span>.</li>\n  <li>Get an <a class=\"gl\" href=\"#gl-abbrbrief\" data-gl=\"abbrbrief\">abbreviated briefing</a> before takeoff if your standard is 1&nbsp;hour or more old&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-3\">[3, para. 8.1]</a></span>.</li>\n  <li>Current conditions drop out of a standard briefing if departure is more than 2&nbsp;hours away, unless you ask&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 3.3.1.1]</a></span>.</li>\n  <li><a class=\"gl\" href=\"#gl-vnr\" data-gl=\"vnr\">VFR flight not recommended</a> is advisory; the decision rests solely with the pilot&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 3.3.1.1]</a></span>.</li>\n  <li>The FAA considers that a <a class=\"gl\" href=\"#gl-selfbrief\" data-gl=\"selfbrief\">self-briefing</a> may be compliant with current regulations&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-3\">[3, para. 7.1]</a></span>.</li>\n  <li>Flight Service briefings, online or voice, are time-stamped and archived for 45&nbsp;days&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 3.3.1]</a></span>.</li>\n  <li>14&nbsp;CFR&nbsp;91.103 requires the PIC to become familiar with all available information before a flight&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-5\">[5]</a></span>.</li>\n  <li><a class=\"gl\" href=\"#gl-fss\" data-gl=\"fss\">Flight Service</a> briefers are certified to interpret weather, not to make original forecasts&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 3.3.2.1]</a></span>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"kf-foot\">Hover, focus, or tap any dotted term for a definition and its official source.</p>\n<div class=\"kf-fade\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></div>\n</details>\n</aside>\n\n<div class=\"hub-body\">\n<p>The rule is short. Under 14&nbsp;CFR&nbsp;91.103, \u201cEach pilot in command shall, before beginning a flight, become familiar with all available information concerning that flight.\u201d For IFR flights and flights not in the vicinity of an airport, that includes \u201cweather reports and forecasts, fuel requirements, alternatives available if the planned flight cannot be completed, and any known traffic delays of which the pilot in command has been advised by ATC\u201d&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-5\">[5, para. (a)]</a></span>. The FAA\u2019s briefing types are how you meet that duty in a consistent way. This page covers the types, what goes in each, and when to get them. <!-- When blog/official-weather-briefing is live (returned HTTP 500 on October 1, 2026), add here: For what 91.103 does and does not require of the source, see \"Is There an Official Weather Briefing?\" --></p>\n\n<h2 id=\"types\">What are the three types of weather briefing?</h2>\n<p>The Handbook says pilots need to know which of the three types fits the flight: standard, abbreviated, or outlook&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 3.3.1]</a></span>. The AIM adds: \u201cPilots should specify to the briefer the type of briefing they want, along with their appropriate background information. This will enable the briefer to tailor the information to the pilot\u2019s intended flight.\u201d&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-2\">[2, para. 7\u20111\u20115]</a></span></p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"stack\" role=\"table\">\n<thead role=\"rowgroup\"><tr role=\"row\"><th scope=\"col\" role=\"columnheader\">Briefing</th><th scope=\"col\" role=\"columnheader\">When to get it</th><th scope=\"col\" role=\"columnheader\">What it gives you</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody role=\"rowgroup\">\n<tr role=\"row\"><th scope=\"row\" role=\"rowheader\"><a class=\"gl\" href=\"#gl-outlookbrief\" data-gl=\"outlookbrief\">Outlook</a></th><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"When to get it\">Departure is 6&nbsp;hours or more away</td><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"What it gives you\">\u201cOutlook briefings provide a general indication of which elements may be a factor during a flight\u201d; planning only&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-3\">[3, para. 7.2.3]</a></span>;&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-4\">[4, para. 5\u20112\u20117]</a></span></td></tr>\n<tr role=\"row\"><th scope=\"row\" role=\"rowheader\"><a class=\"gl\" href=\"#gl-stdbrief\" data-gl=\"stdbrief\">Standard</a></th><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"When to get it\">No previous briefing; within 6&nbsp;hours of departure, and as close to departure as you can</td><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"What it gives you\">\u201cA standard briefing provides a complete weather picture and is the most detailed of all briefings.\u201d&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 3.3.1.1]</a></span>;&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-4\">[4, para. 5\u20112\u20115]</a></span></td></tr>\n<tr role=\"row\"><th scope=\"row\" role=\"rowheader\"><a class=\"gl\" href=\"#gl-abbrbrief\" data-gl=\"abbrbrief\">Abbreviated</a></th><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"When to get it\">Departure delayed, or you need to update an earlier briefing or one or two items</td><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"What it gives you\">The elements that may have changed since your standard briefing&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 3.3.1.2]</a></span>;&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-2\">[2, para. 7\u20111\u20115]</a></span></td></tr>\n<tr role=\"row\"><th scope=\"row\" role=\"rowheader\">Inflight</th><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"When to get it\">You need a briefing or update by radio</td><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"What it gives you\">The same content by type, from Flight Service on frequency&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-2\">[2, para. 7\u20111\u20115]</a></span></td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Flight Service also needs your background: VFR or IFR, aircraft identification and type, departure point and time, altitude, route, destination, and time en route, plus the time of your last briefing if this one is an update&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 3.3.1]</a></span>. All Flight Service briefings, online or voice, \u201care time-stamped and archived for 45&nbsp;days.\u201d&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 3.3.1]</a></span></p>\n\n<h2 id=\"standard\">What is in a standard briefing, and in what order?</h2>\n<p>A standard briefing \u201cincludes conditions and significant weather information that may influence the pilot in planning, altering, or cancelling a proposed route or flight.\u201d The elements come in a set order, though the briefer may combine the first three when that describes conditions better&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 3.3.1.1]</a></span>:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"stack\" role=\"table\">\n<thead role=\"rowgroup\"><tr role=\"row\"><th scope=\"col\" role=\"columnheader\">#</th><th scope=\"col\" role=\"columnheader\">Element</th><th scope=\"col\" role=\"columnheader\">What to listen or look for</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody role=\"rowgroup\">\n<tr role=\"row\"><th scope=\"row\" role=\"rowheader\">1</th><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"Element\">Adverse conditions</td><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"What to listen or look for\">Hazardous weather, airport closures, delays; icing if your aircraft isn\u2019t certified for it&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 3.3.1.1]</a></span></td></tr>\n<tr role=\"row\"><th scope=\"row\" role=\"rowheader\">2</th><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"Element\"><a class=\"gl\" href=\"#gl-vnr\" data-gl=\"vnr\">VFR flight not recommended</a></td><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"What to listen or look for\">The briefer\u2019s judgment that VFR is doubtful. Advisory only; not provided via the internet&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 3.3.1.1]</a></span></td></tr>\n<tr role=\"row\"><th scope=\"row\" role=\"rowheader\">3</th><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"Element\">Synopsis</td><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"What to listen or look for\">Type, location, and movement of <a href=\"/learn/aviation-weather/fronts-troughs-drylines\">weather systems and air masses</a>&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 3.3.1.1]</a></span></td></tr>\n<tr role=\"row\"><th scope=\"row\" role=\"rowheader\">4</th><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"Element\">Current conditions</td><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"What to listen or look for\">Observations, <a class=\"gl\" href=\"#gl-pirep\" data-gl=\"pirep\">PIREPs</a>, satellite, and radar; left out if departure is more than 2&nbsp;hours away unless you ask&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 3.3.1.1]</a></span></td></tr>\n<tr role=\"row\"><th scope=\"row\" role=\"rowheader\">5</th><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"Element\">En route forecast</td><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"What to listen or look for\">In order: departure and climbout, en route, descent; heights MSL unless AGL or CIG&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 3.3.1.1]</a></span></td></tr>\n<tr role=\"row\"><th scope=\"row\" role=\"rowheader\">6</th><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"Element\">Destination forecast</td><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"What to listen or look for\">For your ETA, plus significant changes within 1&nbsp;hour before and after&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 3.3.1.1]</a></span></td></tr>\n<tr role=\"row\"><th scope=\"row\" role=\"rowheader\">7</th><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"Element\">Winds and temperatures aloft</td><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"What to listen or look for\">Knots and tens of degrees, true north, interpolated to your altitude&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 3.3.1.1]</a></span></td></tr>\n<tr role=\"row\"><th scope=\"row\" role=\"rowheader\">8</th><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"Element\">NOTAMs, ATC delays, other information</td><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"What to listen or look for\">Detailed in AIM\u00a07\u20111\u20115&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 3.3.1.1]</a></span>;&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-2\">[2, para. 7\u20111\u20115]</a></span></td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The same order makes a good self-briefing checklist. AC&nbsp;91&#8209;92\u2019s standard briefing checklist also starts from your flight details and then adverse conditions&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-3\">[3, para. 7.4]</a></span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"vnr\">What \u201cVFR flight not recommended\u201d means</h3>\n<p>When the briefer judges that VFR is doubtful, you\u2019ll hear the conditions, the locations, and the phrase \u201cVFR flight not recommended.\u201d \u201cThis recommendation is advisory in nature. The final decision as to whether the flight can be conducted safely rests solely with the pilot.\u201d The Handbook says a non-IFR rated pilot then weighs current and forecast weather against experience and ratings, and the aircraft\u2019s equipment, capabilities, and limitations&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 3.3.1.1]</a></span>. The AIM adds a warning: in minimal VFR weather, conditions can lower unexpectedly beyond your ratings and experience, which can lead to spatial disorientation or loss of control&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-2\">[2, para. 7\u20111\u20115]</a></span>. VNR is not provided via the internet&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 3.3.1.1]</a></span>, so a self-briefing pilot has to make that judgment alone.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"when\">When should you get each briefing? A worked timeline</h2>\n<p>AC&nbsp;91&#8209;92\u2019s \u201cDo\u201d list sets out the order: plan ahead with an outlook, get a standard briefing as close to departure as possible, and get an abbreviated briefing just before takeoff if the standard is 1&nbsp;hour or more old or the weather is questionable&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-3\">[3, para. 8.1]</a></span>. For a typical flight, that looks like this:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"stack\" role=\"table\">\n<thead role=\"rowgroup\"><tr role=\"row\"><th scope=\"col\" role=\"columnheader\">When</th><th scope=\"col\" role=\"columnheader\">What to get</th><th scope=\"col\" role=\"columnheader\">Why</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody role=\"rowgroup\">\n<tr role=\"row\"><th scope=\"row\" role=\"rowheader\">The day before, or 6+ hours out</th><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"What to get\">Outlook briefing</td><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"Why\">Shapes route, altitude, and timing, and ultimately the go/no-go decision&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 3.3.1.3]</a></span>;&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-3\">[3, para. 8.1]</a></span></td></tr>\n<tr role=\"row\"><th scope=\"row\" role=\"rowheader\">Within 6&nbsp;hours, as close to departure as possible</th><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"What to get\">Standard briefing</td><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"Why\">The complete picture for the flight&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-3\">[3, para. 8.1]</a></span>;&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-4\">[4, para. 5\u20112\u20115]</a></span></td></tr>\n<tr role=\"row\"><th scope=\"row\" role=\"rowheader\">Just before takeoff, if the standard is 1&nbsp;hour or more old or the weather is questionable</th><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"What to get\">Abbreviated briefing</td><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"Why\">Catches what changed&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-3\">[3, para. 8.1]</a></span></td></tr>\n<tr role=\"row\"><th scope=\"row\" role=\"rowheader\">In flight</th><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"What to get\">Inflight update from Flight Service or datalink weather</td><td role=\"cell\" data-label=\"Why\">Stay ahead of changes en route&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-2\">[2, para. 7\u20111\u20115]</a></span></td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The outlook matters more than it looks. The Handbook says it can influence route, altitude, and \u201cultimately the \u2018go, no-go\u2019 decision.\u201d&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 3.3.1.3]</a></span> A follow-up briefing is still needed, because an outlook \u201cgenerally only contains information based on weather trends and existing weather in geographical areas at or near the departure airport.\u201d&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 3.3.1.3]</a></span></p>\n\n<h2 id=\"self-brief\">How does self-briefing fit in?</h2>\n<p>AC&nbsp;91&#8209;92 defines a <a class=\"gl\" href=\"#gl-selfbrief\" data-gl=\"selfbrief\">self-briefing</a> as one that \u201cuses all means and sources of reporting, including automation, to provide a thorough evaluation of weather and aeronautical information in advance of flight.\u201d&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-3\">[3, para. 4.19]</a></span> It notes that \u201cmost pilots have become more accustomed to performing a self-briefing than calling an FSS.\u201d&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-3\">[3, para. 7.1]</a></span></p>\n<p>Even if you call, self-brief first. \u201cPilots who prefer to contact Flight Service are encouraged to conduct a self-briefing prior to calling.\u201d&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 3.3.1]</a></span> AC&nbsp;91&#8209;92 shows that an online self-brief can serve as an outlook or a standard briefing, and an abbreviated update can also come from in-cockpit technology&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-3\">[3, para. 7.3]</a></span>. Some online sources don\u2019t include NOTAMs or TFRs, so you may need to find them separately&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 3.3.3]</a></span>.</p>\n<p>Calling Flight Service is still a strong tool for a hard day. The briefer can\u2019t forecast, but can interpret and answer your questions, and \u201cThe briefing is considered complete when the pilot has a clear picture of what weather to expect.\u201d&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 3.3.2.1]</a></span> For the CONUS, Hawaii, and Puerto Rico, call 1-800-WX-BRIEF; for Alaska, 1-833-AK-BRIEF&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 3.3.2.1]</a></span>.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"field-reference\">Where are the official weather briefing references?</h2>\n<section class=\"fieldref\" aria-labelledby=\"field-reference\">\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Start here:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/documentLibrary/media/Advisory_Circular/AC_91-92_Ed_Upd_(3-19-25).pdf\">AC&nbsp;91&#8209;92, Pilot\u2019s Guide to a Preflight Briefing</a>, with its standard briefing checklist&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-3\">[3]</a></span>.</li>\n  <li><a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/sites/faa.gov/files/FAA-H-8083-28B.pdf#page=26\">FAA Aviation Weather Handbook, Section 3.3, Obtaining Weather Information</a>&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1]</a></span>.</li>\n  <li><a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/air_traffic/publications/atpubs/aim_html/chap7_section_1.html\">AIM\u00a07\u20111\u20115, Preflight Briefing</a>&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-2\">[2]</a></span>.</li>\n  <li><a href=\"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-14/chapter-I/subchapter-F/part-91/subpart-B/section-91.103\">14&nbsp;CFR&nbsp;91.103, Preflight action</a>&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-5\">[5]</a></span>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"status\">Links checked October 1, 2026: all returned HTTP 200.</p>\n</section>\n\n<h2>Where do briefings mislead pilots?</h2>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>A stale standard briefing.</strong> AC&nbsp;91&#8209;92\u2019s threshold for an update is 1&nbsp;hour&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-3\">[3, para. 8.1]</a></span>.</li>\n  <li><strong>An outlook treated as a briefing.</strong> It covers trends and weather near departure, not the full route&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 3.3.1.3]</a></span>.</li>\n  <li><strong>No current conditions.</strong> A standard briefing more than 2&nbsp;hours before departure leaves them out unless you ask&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 3.3.1.1]</a></span>.</li>\n  <li><strong>No VNR online.</strong> The VNR statement isn\u2019t provided via the internet&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 3.3.1.1]</a></span>.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>How do you use a briefing in a go/no-go decision?</h2>\n<p>The decision is yours as PIC. Habits drawn from the FAA guidance above:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Brief against your personal minimums.</strong> AC&nbsp;91&#8209;92\u2019s first \u201cDo\u201d is to \u201cEstablish personal minimums that reflect your level of proficiency.\u201d&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-3\">[3, para. 8.1]</a></span> See the <a href=\"/learn/aviation-weather/weather-risk\">weather risk and personal minimums guide</a>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Read adverse conditions first.</strong> They are first in the order because they may change the whole plan&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 3.3.1.1]</a></span>. The <a href=\"/learn/aviation-weather/airmet-sigmet\">AIRMET and SIGMET guide</a> and <a href=\"/learn/aviation-weather/thunderstorms\">thunderstorm guide</a> cover the products.</li>\n  <li><strong>Plan the alternate before you go.</strong> AC&nbsp;91&#8209;92: \u201cHave a contingency plan for alternates if unexpected circumstances arise.\u201d&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-3\">[3, para. 8.1]</a></span></li>\n  <li><strong>Make a final check.</strong> \u201cPilots can make a final weather check immediately before departure using online weather apps, when possible.\u201d&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 3.3.1]</a></span></li>\n</ul>\n<p>More on technique: <a href=\"https://www.planewx.ai/blog/advanced-pilot-weather-briefing\">an advanced weather briefing</a>, <a href=\"https://www.planewx.ai/blog/strategic-planning-vs-tactical-weather-briefing\">strategic planning versus tactical briefing</a>, <a href=\"https://www.planewx.ai/blog/flight-weather-briefer\">working with a flight weather briefer</a>, and <a href=\"https://www.planewx.ai/blog/best-weather-resources-for-private-pilots\">weather resources for private pilots</a>.</p>\n\n<h2>Where does the briefing fit in a disciplined decision?</h2>\n<p>PlaneWX is decision support for general aviation pilots, built around one idea: \u201cFly like it\u2019s your job.\u201d Treat the weather decision the way a professional crew would, with the same process every time. That process is a loop: <span class=\"loop\">Brief, then FRAT, then Fly or Stay, then Debrief</span>, with a Mentor if you want one. Here\u2019s how PlaneWX handles the Brief, as its help center describes&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-6\">[6]</a></span>:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Brief: three views.</strong> Every briefing comes in Quick, Enhanced, and Visual views; Quick is a compact summary of score, gotchas, and key numbers for a fast pre-flight check (<a href=\"https://app.planewx.ai/help/understanding-briefings\">Understanding Your Briefing</a>).</li>\n  <li><strong>Brief: scored to you.</strong> The WX Score is based on your personal minimums and aircraft, shown as Favorable (75 and up), Marginal (50 to 74), or Unfavorable (under 50) (<a href=\"https://app.planewx.ai/help/faqs\">FAQs</a>; <a href=\"https://app.planewx.ai/help/understanding-briefings\">Understanding Your Briefing</a>).</li>\n  <li><strong>Brief: Auto-Brief.</strong> On Pro Plus, when you file an IFR flight plan, PlaneWX sees the filing through FlightAware and creates or refreshes the trip\u2019s briefing (<a href=\"https://app.planewx.ai/help/auto-brief\">Auto-Brief</a>).</li>\n  <li><strong>FRAT.</strong> The flight risk assessment opens in the last 4&nbsp;hours before departure (<a href=\"https://app.planewx.ai/help/frat\">FRAT</a>).</li>\n  <li><strong>Fly or Stay.</strong> \u201cPlaneWX never recommends GO or NO-GO. You make the call as PIC.\u201d (<a href=\"https://app.planewx.ai/help/risk-loop\">The Risk Loop</a>)</li>\n  <li><strong>Debrief.</strong> Compare what the briefing said with what you met. Self Debrief is a PlaneWX Labs feature for Pro Plus (<a href=\"https://app.planewx.ai/help/risk-loop\">The Risk Loop</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The help center is plain about the limits: \u201cPlaneWX is not a substitute for a complete, independent pre-flight weather briefing. Pull one from a source you already trust before you fly.\u201d (<a href=\"https://app.planewx.ai/help/risk-loop\">The Risk Loop</a>)</p>\n\n<!-- Screenshot slots (dark mode; Chief of Staff review required before adding; real trips only, blur tail numbers and names):\n     S1: Quick view of a briefing on a phone.\n     S2: Enhanced view with the adverse conditions section open. -->\n\n<p><a href=\"https://app.planewx.ai/\">Start your next briefing in PlaneWX</a>, or read <a href=\"https://app.planewx.ai/help/understanding-briefings\">how to read a PlaneWX briefing</a>.</p>\n\n<nav class=\"hub-related\" aria-labelledby=\"related-h\">\n<h2 id=\"related-h\">More PlaneWX guides related to weather briefings</h2>\n<ul>\n  <li><a href=\"/learn/aviation-weather/taf\">What Is a TAF? How to Read a Terminal Aerodrome Forecast</a>. The airport forecast, with FM, TEMPO, PROB30, and a full worked decode.</li>\n  <li><a href=\"/learn/aviation-weather/metar\">What Is a METAR? How to Read an Airport Weather Report</a>. The airport observation, every group decoded, plus when a SPECI is issued.</li>\n  <li><a href=\"/learn/aviation-weather/airmet-sigmet\">AIRMET, SIGMET, and CWA: In-Flight Weather Advisories Explained</a>. G-AIRMETs, SIGMETs, Convective SIGMETs, and Center Weather Advisories, with decoded samples.</li>\n  <li><a href=\"/learn/aviation-weather/pirep\">What Is a PIREP? How to Read and Give a Pilot Weather Report</a>. The UA and UUA format, the icing and turbulence scales, and how to give one.</li>\n  <li><a href=\"/learn/aviation-weather/winds-aloft\">Winds and Temperatures Aloft: How to Read an FB Forecast</a>. The FB code decoded, including winds over 100&nbsp;knots, and how model winds differ.</li>\n  <li><a href=\"/learn/aviation-weather/icing\">Icing Forecasts: CIP, FIP, Freezing Level, and Icing Intensity</a>. What the icing products show, what their intensity words mean, and where they fall short.</li>\n  <li><a href=\"/learn/aviation-weather/turbulence\">Turbulence Forecasts: GTG, GTG-N, and the Intensity Scale</a>. The automated turbulence forecast and nowcast, EDR, and the advisories that cover what GTG does not.</li>\n  <li><a href=\"/learn/aviation-weather/weather-radar\">Weather Radar for Pilots: NEXRAD, TDWR, Reflectivity, and Datalink Delay</a>. How to read radar images, their blind spots, and why cockpit mosaics are older than they look.</li>\n  <li><a href=\"/learn/aviation-weather/density-altitude\">Density Altitude Calculator and Guide: What It Is and How to Calculate It</a>. The FAA definition, the formulas, a worked example, a calculator, and the Koch chart.</li>\n  <li><a href=\"/learn/flight-risk-assessment-tool\">Flight Risk Assessment Tool (FRAT): What It Is and How to Use One Honestly</a>. Where the FAA FRAT comes from, how the scores work, and how to fill one out without fooling yourself.</li>\n  <li><a href=\"/learn/aviation-weather/thunderstorms\">Thunderstorms for Pilots: Hazards, Avoidance, Outlooks, and Watches</a>. Why every thunderstorm is a hazard, the 20-mile rule, and the convective outlook, TCF, ECFP, and watch products.</li>\n  <li><a href=\"/learn/aviation-weather/ceiling-visibility\">Ceiling, Visibility, and Flight Categories: VFR, MVFR, IFR, and LIFR</a>. What counts as a ceiling, how visibility is measured, and why flight categories are not VFR minimums.</li>\n  <li><a href=\"/learn/aviation-weather/fog\">Fog for Pilots: Radiation, Advection, Upslope, and Freezing Fog</a>. How each type of fog forms and clears, how it is reported and forecast, and what to do when the spread is small.</li>\n  <li><a href=\"/learn/aviation-weather/wind-shear-microburst\">Low-Level Wind Shear and Microbursts: Recognition, Reports, and Escape</a>. The LLWS definition, microburst structure, the alerts you hear on ATIS, and the FAA escape guidance.</li>\n  <li><a href=\"/learn/aviation-weather/mountain-wave\">Mountain Wave and Mountain Weather: Lee Waves, Rotors, and Downslope Winds</a>. When mountain waves form, the clouds that mark them, rotor and downslope hazards, and how to plan a crossing.</li>\n  <li><a href=\"/learn/aviation-weather/weather-risk\">Weather Risk and Personal Minimums: FAA Definitions and Where to Go Next</a>. Hazard, risk, and personal minimums as the FAA defines them, with links to the PlaneWX guides that go deeper.</li>\n</ul>\n</nav>\n\n<section class=\"faq\">\n<h2>Weather briefing FAQ: common pilot questions</h2>\n\n<h3>What are the three types of weather briefings?</h3>\n<p>Standard, abbreviated, and outlook&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 3.3.1]</a></span>. \u201cPilots should specify to the briefer the type of briefing they want, along with their appropriate background information.\u201d&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-2\">[2, para. 7\u20111\u20115]</a></span></p>\n\n<h3>When should I get an outlook briefing?</h3>\n<p>\u201cAn outlook briefing may be requested when a planned departure is six or more hours away.\u201d It is for planning, and a follow-up standard or abbreviated briefing before departure is advisable&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 3.3.1.3]</a></span>.</p>\n\n<h3>When should I get a standard briefing?</h3>\n<p>When you are planning a flight and haven\u2019t had a previous briefing or preliminary information online&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-2\">[2, para. 7\u20111\u20115]</a></span>. AC&nbsp;91&#8209;92 says to get it \u201cas close to your departure time as possible.\u201d&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-3\">[3, para. 8.1]</a></span></p>\n\n<h3>When should I get an abbreviated briefing?</h3>\n<p>To update an earlier briefing or get specific items. AC&nbsp;91&#8209;92: \u201cObtain an abbreviated briefing just before takeoff if your standard briefing is 1&nbsp;hour or more old or if the weather is questionable.\u201d&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-3\">[3, para. 8.1]</a></span></p>\n\n<h3>What does \u201cVFR flight not recommended\u201d mean?</h3>\n<p>The briefer judges that VFR flight is doubtful. \u201cThis recommendation is advisory in nature. The final decision as to whether the flight can be conducted safely rests solely with the pilot.\u201d&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 3.3.1.1]</a></span></p>\n\n<h3>Can I brief myself online instead of calling Flight Service?</h3>\n<p>Yes. \u201cThe FAA considers that a self-briefing may be compliant with current Federal aviation regulations.\u201d&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-3\">[3, para. 7.1]</a></span> The Handbook encourages a self-briefing even if you plan to call&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 3.3.1]</a></span>.</p>\n\n<h3>What is the Flight Service phone number?</h3>\n<p>For the CONUS, Hawaii, and Puerto Rico, 1-800-WX-BRIEF (1-800-992-7433). For Alaska, 1-833-AK-BRIEF (1-833-252-7433)&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 3.3.2.1]</a></span>.</p>\n\n<h3>Can a Flight Service briefer forecast the weather?</h3>\n<p>No. \u201cThey are not authorized to make original forecasts\u201d; they translate and interpret the available weather information for your route&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-1\">[1, para. 3.3.2.1]</a></span>.</p>\n\n<h3>Does PlaneWX meet 14&nbsp;CFR&nbsp;91.103?</h3>\n<p>The PlaneWX FAQ says it \u201cprovides weather information from federal regulatory agencies (NOAA, NWS, FAA) to help pilots meet the requirement to obtain all available information before a flight.\u201d It adds: \u201cThere is no regulatory requirement to obtain a briefing from any specific source; FAA Advisory Circular 91-92 endorses pilot self-briefing.\u201d&nbsp;<span class=\"cites\"><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-6\">[6]</a></span> You remain PIC and make the final decision.</p>\n</section>\n\n<section class=\"glossary\" id=\"glossary\" aria-labelledby=\"glossary-h\">\n<h2 id=\"glossary-h\">Weather briefing glossary of terms</h2>\n<p>Every term below links to the FAA, NWS, or NOAA document that defines it. The same definitions appear when you hover, focus, or tap a dotted term on this page.</p>\n<dl>\n  <dt id=\"gl-abbrbrief\"><dfn>Abbreviated briefing</dfn></dt>\n  <dd>A shortened briefing to update an earlier one or to supply only the items a pilot asks for. <span class=\"src\">Source: <a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/sites/faa.gov/files/FAA-H-8083-28B.pdf#page=27\">FAA Aviation Weather Handbook, 3.3.1.2</a></span></dd>\n  <dt id=\"gl-fss\"><dfn>FSS</dfn> (Flight Service Station)</dt>\n  <dd>The FAA flight service that provides preflight and in-flight briefings and flight plan filing to pilots. <span class=\"src\">Source: <a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/sites/faa.gov/files/FAA-H-8083-28B.pdf#page=28\">FAA Aviation Weather Handbook, 3.3.2.1</a></span></dd>\n  <dt id=\"gl-outlookbrief\"><dfn>Outlook briefing</dfn></dt>\n  <dd>A general planning briefing for a departure six or more hours away, to be followed by a standard briefing. <span class=\"src\">Source: <a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/sites/faa.gov/files/FAA-H-8083-28B.pdf#page=27\">FAA Aviation Weather Handbook, 3.3.1.3</a></span></dd>\n  <dt id=\"gl-pirep\"><dfn>PIREP</dfn> (Pilot Weather Report)</dt>\n  <dd>A report of weather a pilot actually encountered in flight, shared to help other pilots and forecasters. <span class=\"src\">Source: <a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/sites/faa.gov/files/FAA-H-8083-28B.pdf#page=310\">FAA Aviation Weather Handbook, 24.5.1</a></span></dd>\n  <dt id=\"gl-selfbrief\"><dfn>Self-briefing</dfn></dt>\n  <dd>A preflight briefing a pilot builds from all available sources, including automation, without a briefer. <span class=\"src\">Source: <a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/documentLibrary/media/Advisory_Circular/AC_91-92_Ed_Upd_(3-19-25).pdf#page=4\">FAA AC&nbsp;91&#8209;92, 4.19</a></span></dd>\n  <dt id=\"gl-stdbrief\"><dfn>Standard briefing</dfn></dt>\n  <dd>The most complete and detailed preflight briefing, for a flight departing within about six hours. <span class=\"src\">Source: <a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/sites/faa.gov/files/FAA-H-8083-28B.pdf#page=26\">FAA Aviation Weather Handbook, 3.3.1.1</a></span></dd>\n  <dt id=\"gl-vnr\"><dfn>VNR</dfn> (VFR flight not recommended)</dt>\n  <dd>A briefer\u2019s advisory that conditions make VFR flight questionable; the decision stays with the pilot. <span class=\"src\">Source: <a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/air_traffic/publications/atpubs/aim_html/chap7_section_1.html\">AIM, 7&#8209;1&#8209;5</a></span></dd>\n</dl>\n</section>\n\n<p class=\"note\">This page explains published FAA guidance on briefing types. It is not legal advice and not a substitute for a current weather briefing. Always get a current briefing before you fly.</p>\n\n<section class=\"refs\" aria-labelledby=\"references\">\n<h2 id=\"references\">Weather briefing references: sources cited in this guide</h2>\n<ol>\n  <li id=\"ref-1\">FAA&#8209;H&#8209;8083&#8209;28B, \u201cAviation Weather Handbook.\u201d Federal Aviation Administration, Flight Standards Service. April 2, 2026. Paragraphs cited: 3.3.1 to 3.3.1.3, 3.3.2.1, 3.3.3. <a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/regulationspolicies/handbooksmanuals/aviation/faa-h-8083-28b-aviation-weather-handbook\">https://www.faa.gov/regulationspolicies/handbooksmanuals/aviation/faa-h-8083-28b-aviation-weather-handbook</a>. PDF: <a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/sites/faa.gov/files/FAA-H-8083-28B.pdf\">https://www.faa.gov/sites/faa.gov/files/FAA-H-8083-28B.pdf</a></li>\n  <li id=\"ref-2\">\u201c7&#8209;1&#8209;5. Preflight Briefing.\u201d Aeronautical Information Manual (AIM), Chapter 7, Section 1. Federal Aviation Administration. Effective July 9, 2026 (Change 3). <a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/air_traffic/publications/atpubs/aim_html/chap7_section_1.html\">https://www.faa.gov/air_traffic/publications/atpubs/aim_html/chap7_section_1.html</a></li>\n  <li id=\"ref-3\">AC&nbsp;91&#8209;92, \u201cPilot\u2019s Guide to a Preflight Briefing.\u201d Federal Aviation Administration. March 15, 2021 (editorial update March 19, 2025). Paragraphs cited: 4.19, 7.1, 7.2.3, 7.3, 7.4, 8.1. <a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/documentLibrary/media/Advisory_Circular/AC_91-92_Ed_Upd_(3-19-25).pdf\">https://www.faa.gov/documentLibrary/media/Advisory_Circular/AC_91-92_Ed_Upd_(3-19-25).pdf</a></li>\n  <li id=\"ref-4\">FAA Order JO\u00a07110.10EE, \u201cFlight Services,\u201d with Changes 1, 2, and 3. Federal Aviation Administration, Air Traffic Organization. Effective February 20, 2025; Change 3 dated July 9, 2026. Paragraphs cited: 5&#8209;2&#8209;5, 5&#8209;2&#8209;7. <a href=\"https://www.faa.gov/documentLibrary/media/Order/7110.10EE_Bsc_w_Chg_1_2_and_3_dtd_7-9-26.pdf\">https://www.faa.gov/documentLibrary/media/Order/7110.10EE_Bsc_w_Chg_1_2_and_3_dtd_7-9-26.pdf</a></li>\n  <li id=\"ref-5\">14&nbsp;CFR&nbsp;91.103, \u201cPreflight action.\u201d Electronic Code of Federal Regulations. Accessed October 1, 2026. <a href=\"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-14/chapter-I/subchapter-F/part-91/subpart-B/section-91.103\">https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-14/chapter-I/subchapter-F/part-91/subpart-B/section-91.103</a></li>\n  <li id=\"ref-6\">PlaneWX Help Center: \u201cUnderstanding Your Briefing,\u201d \u201cFrequently Asked Questions,\u201d \u201cAuto-Brief,\u201d \u201cFRAT,\u201d \u201cThe Risk Loop.\u201d PlaneWX. Accessed October 1, 2026. <a href=\"https://app.planewx.ai/help/understanding-briefings\">Understanding Your Briefing</a> \u00b7 <a href=\"https://app.planewx.ai/help/faqs\">FAQs</a> \u00b7 <a href=\"https://app.planewx.ai/help/auto-brief\">Auto-Brief</a> \u00b7 <a href=\"https://app.planewx.ai/help/frat\">FRAT</a> \u00b7 <a href=\"https://app.planewx.ai/help/risk-loop\">The Risk Loop</a></li>\n</ol>\n</section>\n</div>\n\n</article>"
export const INLINE_STYLE = "\n  :root {\n    --bg: #ffffff; --fg: #1b1f24; --muted: #57606a; --line: #d0d7de;\n    --card: #f6f8fa; --accent: #0b5cad; --quote: #eef4fb;\n  }\n  @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {\n    :root {\n      --bg: #0f1419; --fg: #e6e8eb; --muted: #9aa4af; --line: #2d3640;\n      --card: #161d24; --accent: #6cb4ff; --quote: #142231;\n    }\n  }\n  * { box-sizing: border-box; }\n  html { -webkit-text-size-adjust: 100%; }\n  body { margin: 0; background: var(--bg); color: var(--fg);\n    font: 18px/1.65 -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, \"Segoe UI\", Roboto, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif; }\n  main { max-width: 760px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 32px 20px 64px; }\n  h1 { font-size: 2.05rem; line-height: 1.2; margin: 0 0 12px; }\n  h2 { font-size: 1.45rem; line-height: 1.3; margin: 44px 0 12px; padding-top: 8px; border-top: 1px solid var(--line); }\n  h3 { font-size: 1.12rem; margin: 26px 0 8px; }\n  p, ul, ol { margin: 0 0 16px; }\n  li { margin: 0 0 6px; }\n  a { color: var(--accent); }\n  .byline { color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.92rem; margin-bottom: 24px; }\n  .answer { background: var(--card); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-left: 4px solid var(--accent);\n    border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px 18px; font-size: 1.06rem; margin: 0 0 20px; }\n  .takeaways { background: var(--card); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px 4px; margin: 0 0 24px; }\n  .takeaways h2 { border: 0; margin: 0 0 8px; padding: 0; font-size: 1.05rem; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .04em; color: var(--muted); }\n  blockquote { margin: 0 0 18px; padding: 12px 16px; background: var(--quote); border-left: 4px solid var(--accent); border-radius: 6px; }\n  blockquote p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }\n  blockquote footer { color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: 6px; }\n  .cite { font-size: 0.78em; vertical-align: super; line-height: 0; text-decoration: none; white-space: nowrap; }\n  .cite:hover { text-decoration: underline; }\n  .cite + .cite { margin-left: 0.25em; }\n  table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin: 0 0 20px; font-size: 0.95rem; }\n  th, td { border: 1px solid var(--line); padding: 10px 12px; vertical-align: top; text-align: left; }\n  th { background: var(--card); }\n  .checklist { background: var(--card); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px 6px; margin: 0 0 20px; }\n  .checklist h3 { margin-top: 10px; }\n  .checklist ul { list-style: none; padding-left: 0; }\n  .checklist li::before { content: \"\\2610\"; margin-right: 8px; color: var(--muted); }\n  .faq h3 { margin-top: 22px; }\n  .note { color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.95rem; font-style: italic; }\n  .refs ol { padding-left: 1.4em; font-size: 0.93rem; }\n  .refs li { margin-bottom: 10px; word-break: break-word; }\n  .refs li:target { background: var(--quote); outline: 2px solid var(--accent); border-radius: 4px; }\n  .loop { font-weight: 600; }\n  /* ---- Hub layout: header, Key Facts sidebar, body (PlaneWX /learn/aviation-weather template) ---- */\n  main.hub-main { max-width: 1120px; }\n  .hub { display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); gap: 0 40px; }\n  .hub-head, .keyfacts, .hub-body { min-width: 0; }\n  /* ---- Sub-headings: real h3, semibold, about 1.13x body, clear space above ---- */\n  .hub h3 { font-size: 1.13em; font-weight: 600; line-height: 1.35; margin: 36px 0 10px; }\n  .hub .faq h3 { margin-top: 32px; }\n  .hub .fieldref h3 { margin-top: 4px; }\n  /* ---- Citations stay on the line of the word before them ---- */\n  /* A cite group never breaks inside, at any width (including Key Facts). Groups too long for the narrowest\n     container are split in the source so each cite sits right after its claim. */\n  .cites { white-space: nowrap; }\n  .cite + .cite { margin-left: 0; }  /* no gap before a cite that follows a cite */\n  /* ---- Key Facts ---- */\n  .kf-box { background: var(--card); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-top: 4px solid var(--accent);\n    border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px 4px; margin: 0 0 24px; font-size: 0.86rem; line-height: 1.42; }\n  .kf-box summary { list-style: none; cursor: pointer; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; }\n  .kf-box summary::-webkit-details-marker { display: none; }\n  .kf-box summary::after { content: \"Show\"; font-size: 0.8rem; color: var(--accent); font-weight: 600; }\n  .kf-box[open] summary::after { content: \"Hide\"; }\n  .kf-box h2 { border: 0; margin: 0; padding: 0; font-size: 1rem; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .04em; color: var(--muted); }\n  .kf-box ol { margin: 8px 0 6px; padding-left: 1.25em; }\n  .kf-box li { margin: 0 0 6px; }\n  .kf-box .kf-foot { color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.78rem; margin: 2px 0 8px; }\n  .kf-fade { display: none; }\n  /* ---- Official field reference box ---- */\n  .fieldref { background: var(--quote); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-left: 4px solid var(--accent); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px 6px; margin: 0 0 22px; }\n  .fieldref .status { color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.85rem; }\n  /* ---- Raw code blocks (TAF, METAR, etc.) ---- */\n  .raw-wrap { position: relative; margin: 0 0 8px; }\n  pre.taf-raw { background: var(--card); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 16px; overflow-x: auto; margin: 0;\n    font: 0.95rem/1.9 ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, Consolas, \"Liberation Mono\", monospace;\n    scrollbar-width: thin; scrollbar-color: var(--muted) var(--line); }\n  pre.taf-raw::-webkit-scrollbar { height: 8px; }\n  pre.taf-raw::-webkit-scrollbar-track { background: var(--line); border-radius: 0 0 8px 8px; }\n  pre.taf-raw::-webkit-scrollbar-thumb { background: var(--muted); border-radius: 4px; }\n  pre.taf-raw:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--accent); outline-offset: 2px; }\n  pre.taf-raw .tk { color: inherit; }\n  .scroll-note { display: none; }\n  .decode-cap { color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.88rem; margin: 0 0 18px; }\n  .table-intro { color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.92rem; margin: 0 0 8px; text-align: left; }\n  /* ---- Tables ---- */\n  table.decode td:first-child { font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, Consolas, \"Liberation Mono\", monospace; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }\n  table.decode tr:target td { background: var(--quote); }\n  table.times td, table.times th { white-space: nowrap; }\n  table.times th { white-space: normal; }\n  .table-wrap { overflow-x: auto; margin: 0 0 20px; }\n  .table-wrap table { margin: 0; }\n  code { font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, Consolas, \"Liberation Mono\", monospace; font-size: 0.92em; }\n  /* ---- On-page glossary (no-JS fallback and tooltip anchor targets) ---- */\n  .glossary dl { margin: 0 0 16px; }\n  .glossary dt { font-size: 1rem; font-weight: 600; margin-top: 14px; }\n  .glossary dfn { font-style: normal; }\n  .glossary dt:target, .glossary dt:target + dd { background: var(--quote); }\n  .glossary dd { margin: 2px 0 0; font-size: 0.95rem; }\n  .glossary .src { color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.85rem; }\n  /* ---- Below 600 px: stacked-card tables, scroll hint and fade on raw blocks ---- */\n  @media (max-width: 599.98px) {\n    table.stack, table.stack tbody, table.stack tr, table.stack td, table.stack th[scope=\"row\"] { display: block; width: 100%; }\n    table.stack thead { position: absolute; width: 1px; height: 1px; overflow: hidden; clip: rect(0 0 0 0); clip-path: inset(50%); white-space: nowrap; }\n    table.stack tr { border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 8px; margin: 0 0 12px; overflow: hidden; }\n    table.stack td, table.stack th[scope=\"row\"] { border: 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--line); padding: 8px 12px; }\n    table.stack tr > :first-child { border-top: 0; }\n    table.stack th[scope=\"row\"], table.decode td:first-child { background: var(--card); font-weight: 600; }\n    table.stack td[data-label]::before { content: attr(data-label); display: block; font: 600 0.74rem/1.4 -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, \"Segoe UI\", Roboto, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif;\n      text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .04em; color: var(--muted); margin-bottom: 2px; }\n    table.decode td:first-child::before { display: none; }\n    .table-wrap:has(table.stack) { overflow: visible; }\n    .scroll-note { display: block; color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.82rem; margin: 0 0 6px; }\n    .raw-wrap::after { content: \"\"; position: absolute; top: 1px; right: 1px; bottom: 9px; width: 40px; pointer-events: none;\n      border-radius: 0 8px 0 0; background: linear-gradient(to right, rgba(0,0,0,0), var(--card)); transition: opacity .15s; }\n    .raw-wrap.at-end::after { opacity: 0; }\n    table.times { font-size: 0.88rem; }\n    table.times th, table.times td { padding: 8px 7px; }\n  }\n  /* ---- Desktop: two columns, sticky Key Facts (kept last so it overrides the mobile toggle styles) ---- */\n  @media (min-width: 980px) {\n    .hub { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 760px) 320px; justify-content: center; }\n    .hub-head { grid-column: 1; grid-row: 1; }\n    .hub-body { grid-column: 1; grid-row: 2; }\n    .keyfacts { grid-column: 2; grid-row: 1 / span 2; }\n    /* Capped only when taller than the viewport; a bottom fade shows while more facts are below. */\n    .keyfacts .kf-box { position: sticky; top: 16px; max-height: calc(100vh - 32px); overflow-y: auto; }\n    .keyfacts summary { pointer-events: none; }\n    .keyfacts .kf-box summary::after, .keyfacts .kf-box[open] summary::after { content: none; }\n    .kf-box.is-capped .kf-fade { display: block; position: sticky; bottom: -4px; height: 36px; margin: -36px -16px 0; pointer-events: none;\n      background: linear-gradient(to bottom, rgba(0,0,0,0), var(--card)); }\n    .kf-box.is-capped.at-end .kf-fade { opacity: 0; }\n  }\n  /* ---- Glossary tooltip (hover, keyboard focus, tap). No framework. ---- */\n  a.gl { color: inherit; text-decoration: underline dotted; text-decoration-thickness: 1px; text-underline-offset: 3px; cursor: help; }\n  a.gl:hover, a.gl[aria-expanded=\"true\"] { color: var(--accent); text-decoration-style: solid; }\n  a.gl:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--accent); outline-offset: 2px; border-radius: 2px; }\n  .gl-tip { position: fixed; z-index: 1000; max-width: min(340px, calc(100vw - 24px)); background: var(--bg); color: var(--fg);\n    border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 8px; box-shadow: 0 6px 24px rgba(0,0,0,.18); padding: 10px 12px;\n    font: 0.88rem/1.5 -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, \"Segoe UI\", Roboto, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif; white-space: normal; text-align: left; }\n  .gl-tip[hidden] { display: none; }\n  .gl-tip .gl-t { display: block; font-weight: 700; }\n  .gl-tip .gl-x { display: block; color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.82rem; margin-bottom: 4px; }\n  .gl-tip .gl-d { display: block; margin-bottom: 6px; }\n  .gl-tip .gl-s { display: block; font-size: 0.8rem; }\n  .gl-tip .gl-s a { color: var(--accent); }\n  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) { .gl-tip { transition: opacity .12s; } }\n"
